1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,281
This detailed local training course explains how to discuss Spatial Methods Workbench outputs with scientific collaborators.

2
00:00:09,281 --> 00:00:17,365
It is not an automated interpretation service, clinical guidance, or a substitute for the full publications.

3
00:00:17,365 --> 00:00:36,975
For every workflow, we will identify the statistical estimand, the quantities reported by the Workbench or source method, the uncertainty or validation needed, the kind of biological hypothesis the result may motivate, and the claims the result cannot establish.

4
00:00:36,975 --> 00:00:45,956
Always begin with the observational unit, sampling hierarchy, preprocessing, coordinate system, and scientific question.

5
00:00:46,306 --> 00:00:48,866
Use five layers in this order.

6
00:00:48,866 --> 00:00:59,616
First, state what one observation represents, how observations are nested, and how raw measurements became the analysis table.

7
00:00:59,616 --> 00:01:03,370
Second, name the estimand in plain language.

8
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:12,158
Third, describe uncertainty, permutation, posterior selection, bootstrap, held-out error, or stability.

9
00:01:12,158 --> 00:01:20,860
Fourth, state a biological hypothesis using cautious language such as consistent with or may motivate.

10
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:33,913
Fifth, name technical and biological alternatives and the replication, perturbation, orthogonal assay, or prospective design needed for a stronger claim.

11
00:01:34,263 --> 00:01:43,285
Interpretation fails when the analyst starts from the result table rather than the data-generating process.

12
00:01:43,285 --> 00:01:50,030
Identify the unit represented by a row and the independent biological replicate.

13
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:56,606
Describe nesting within patients, specimens, slides, sites, times, or volumes.

14
00:01:56,606 --> 00:02:04,531
Reconstruct segmentation, registration, normalization, transformations, masks, and exclusions.

15
00:02:04,531 --> 00:02:15,154
For spatial and volumetric data, record coordinate units, tissue boundaries, anisotropy, section spacing, and missing regions.

16
00:02:15,154 --> 00:02:26,789
A statistically correct calculation can still answer the wrong scientific question if the sampling hierarchy or geometry is misunderstood.

17
00:02:27,139 --> 00:02:31,132
Moran's I is a global spatial autocorrelation statistic.

18
00:02:31,132 --> 00:02:40,330
It compares weighted cross-products of centered observations with overall squared variation, scaled by the spatial weight matrix.

19
00:02:40,330 --> 00:02:48,957
The graph is therefore part of the estimand: an eight-neighbor result is not the same quantity as a distance-band result.

20
00:02:48,957 --> 00:03:02,433
Positive I indicates neighboring similarity under that graph, negative I indicates neighboring dissimilarity, and values near the randomization expectation indicate little global structure.

21
00:03:02,433 --> 00:03:08,922
It summarizes the full domain and does not locate the specific region creating the pattern.

22
00:03:09,272 --> 00:03:17,798
The permutation p-value measures how extreme the observed statistic is under the chosen label-exchange scheme.

23
00:03:17,798 --> 00:03:21,131
Its credibility depends on exchangeability.

24
00:03:21,131 --> 00:03:31,905
If rows share a patient, slide, specimen, batch, region, or time point, unrestricted permutation can create a false reference distribution.

25
00:03:31,905 --> 00:03:38,493
Report the statistic, graph, sample count, number of permutations, seed, and p-value.

26
00:03:38,493 --> 00:03:43,763
Repeat plausible graphs and examine boundaries and sampling density.

27
00:03:43,763 --> 00:03:52,057
A biologically disciplined statement is that the feature is spatially structured under the specified graph.

28
00:03:52,057 --> 00:03:57,328
Do not infer a cellular mechanism from global autocorrelation alone.

29
00:03:57,678 --> 00:04:12,170
The conditional-network screen estimates a covariance matrix, applies a ridge penalty, inverts it to obtain a precision matrix, and standardizes the off-diagonal precision entries as partial correlations.

30
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:22,045
A partial correlation is the residual linear association between two included variables after conditioning on the other included variables.

31
00:04:22,045 --> 00:04:32,061
The ridge penalty is not merely numerical housekeeping; it changes the estimate, especially with correlated features or limited observations.

32
00:04:32,061 --> 00:04:36,679
The graph threshold is a decision applied to a continuous matrix.

33
00:04:36,679 --> 00:04:42,504
Preserve and inspect the matrix rather than interpreting only the displayed edges.

34
00:04:42,854 --> 00:04:52,611
A positive edge describes conditional coordination and a negative edge conditional opposition under the fitted linear model.

35
00:04:52,611 --> 00:05:00,401
An absent displayed edge means the estimate fell below the chosen rule, not that biology is absent.

36
00:05:00,401 --> 00:05:07,168
An edge may motivate hypotheses about a shared niche, exclusion, or coordinated state.

37
00:05:07,168 --> 00:05:14,013
It does not establish physical contact, signaling, regulation, direction, or causation.

38
00:05:14,013 --> 00:05:24,950
Review transformations, outliers, feature panel composition, ridge sensitivity, sample size, zone sizes, thresholds, and omitted variables.

39
00:05:24,950 --> 00:05:30,537
Report the continuous estimate with the exact model and threshold used.

40
00:05:30,887 --> 00:05:43,238
The ISPat publication first characterizes spatial intensity surfaces and then uses multi-study factor structure to separate shared from region-specific covariance patterns.

41
00:05:43,238 --> 00:05:59,180
Publication-level summaries include shared and zone-specific covariance or loading quantities, partial-correlation matrices, thresholded positive and negative edges, and proportions of samples in which an edge sign occurs.

42
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:05,356
In simulations, recovery is also summarized with an RV coefficient comparing matrices.

43
00:06:05,356 --> 00:06:14,763
These quantities address spatially adjusted conditional organization; they are richer than a separate network fit within each zone.

44
00:06:15,113 --> 00:06:28,290
Explain a shared pattern as conditional organization conserved across the modeled zones, and a zone-specific pattern as a deviation associated with a prespecified domain.

45
00:06:28,290 --> 00:06:41,931
An across-sample edge proportion summarizes how often a sign is recovered under the paper's aggregation rule; it is not the posterior probability of a universal biological law.

46
00:06:41,931 --> 00:06:46,116
Thresholded edge signs summarize continuous estimates.

47
00:06:46,116 --> 00:06:56,890
In the pancreatic application, tumor-intensity zones and epithelial, APC, CTL, T-helper, and Treg features provided the biological context.

48
00:06:56,890 --> 00:07:02,470
For a new assay, those labels and zones must be independently justified.

49
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:11,496
Before interpreting ISPat biology, verify phenotype or marker definitions, normalization, and spatial units.

50
00:07:11,496 --> 00:07:21,296
Ensure zones were defined independently of the desired result and have adequate observations across biological replicates.

51
00:07:21,296 --> 00:07:30,132
Inspect the fitted intensity surfaces, continuous matrices, thresholded signs, and sample-level heterogeneity.

52
00:07:30,132 --> 00:07:39,691
Review prior choices, chain behavior, effective sample size, convergence, and sensitivity to thresholds and bandwidths.

53
00:07:39,691 --> 00:07:45,154
The ISPat authors describe the networks as conditional dependencies.

54
00:07:45,154 --> 00:07:53,026
Functional validation, orthogonal assays, and replication remain necessary for mechanistic claims.

55
00:07:53,376 --> 00:08:01,524
GP-GHS represents a candidate conditional-dependence edge as a spatially varying field through basis coefficients.

56
00:08:01,524 --> 00:08:14,032
Grouped horseshoe shrinkage acts jointly on the coefficients for an edge, allowing the method to suppress unsupported edge functions while retaining spatially structured ones.

57
00:08:14,032 --> 00:08:20,179
A Workbench adjacency is a compact summary of the configured posterior selection rule.

58
00:08:20,179 --> 00:08:26,541
It does not fully describe the edge's sign, magnitude, uncertainty, or spatial variation.

59
00:08:26,541 --> 00:08:36,118
Those properties live in the detailed fitted object and must be inspected at locations or regions relevant to the scientific question.

60
00:08:36,468 --> 00:08:42,076
A selected GP-GHS edge does not imply the same sign or magnitude everywhere.

61
00:08:42,076 --> 00:08:55,062
Review basis dimension, smoothness, coordinate scaling, tissue boundaries, chain mixing, effective sample sizes, convergence statistics, selection threshold, and symmetry rule.

62
00:08:55,062 --> 00:09:04,432
Map the detailed posterior edge field with uncertainty and compare results across reasonable settings and biological specimens.

63
00:09:04,432 --> 00:09:12,253
Appropriate language is that the model found evidence for a spatially structured conditional relationship.

64
00:09:12,253 --> 00:09:23,690
Regional biological labels should be applied only after independent annotation and should not turn a conditional association into a direction or mechanism.

65
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:33,218
ISPat 3D extends marker-specific spatial adjustment and multi-study network estimation to registered volumes or serial sections.

66
00:09:33,218 --> 00:09:46,340
Its scientific quantities include three-dimensional spatial trends and shared or zone-specific covariance and partial-correlation structure, retained within per-volume fitted objects.

67
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:58,315
A shared volumetric edge describes conditional organization conserved across the modeled domains, while a zone-specific edge describes a modeled deviation in a domain.

68
00:09:58,315 --> 00:10:05,772
The z axis and volume identifiers are scientific variables, not merely columns needed to pass readiness.

69
00:10:06,122 --> 00:10:10,616
Three-dimensional interpretation begins with geometry.

70
00:10:10,616 --> 00:10:22,851
Verify orientation, section order, thickness, spacing, anisotropy, registration error, missing sections, masks, deformations, and coordinate units.

71
00:10:22,851 --> 00:10:27,512
Examine staining drift and heterogeneity across volumes.

72
00:10:27,512 --> 00:10:39,331
A depth-dependent edge can arise from real volumetric organization, but also from misregistration, discontinuous sampling, or technical drift.

73
00:10:39,331 --> 00:10:48,403
Review the spatial adjustment and full matrices within each volume, and replicate across independent volumes.

74
00:10:48,403 --> 00:10:54,978
State a volumetric conditional pattern before proposing a biological mechanism.

75
00:10:55,328 --> 00:11:07,867
For continuous outcomes, RMSE emphasizes large errors, MAE reports average absolute error, and held-out R squared compares predictive variation under the test design.

76
00:11:07,867 --> 00:11:21,842
For binary outcomes, predicted probabilities are primary; accuracy depends on the cutoff and prevalence and should be supplemented with discrimination and calibration where appropriate.

77
00:11:21,842 --> 00:11:29,093
Coefficients are conditional predictive associations under the fitted model, not causal effects.

78
00:11:29,093 --> 00:11:33,701
Every metric is conditional on the test population and split.

79
00:11:33,701 --> 00:11:42,010
Report its denominator, uncertainty, prevalence, and any preprocessing learned exclusively from training data.

80
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:45,916
Spatial and biomedical records are structured.

81
00:11:45,916 --> 00:11:59,214
If neighboring locations or observations from one patient or slide occur in both training and test sets, the model can appear to generalize while memorizing shared context.

82
00:11:59,214 --> 00:12:05,863
Use patient, specimen, slide, site, time, or spatial blocks appropriate to deployment.

83
00:12:05,863 --> 00:12:11,971
Perform normalization, feature selection, and tuning inside the training folds.

84
00:12:11,971 --> 00:12:20,553
Compare with transparent baselines, quantify calibration and subgroup performance, and seek an external cohort.

85
00:12:20,553 --> 00:12:25,192
Predictive accuracy does not explain why the outcome occurs.

86
00:12:25,542 --> 00:12:37,910
The spatial clustering path performs k-means in a constructed space containing standardized biological features and coordinates multiplied by the selected spatial weight.

87
00:12:37,910 --> 00:12:46,495
The weight changes the question: zero emphasizes feature similarity, while larger values favor geographic compactness.

88
00:12:46,495 --> 00:12:56,680
Assignments and centers describe the algorithmic partition, and the between-to-total sum-of-squares ratio is an internal separation measure.

89
00:12:56,680 --> 00:13:01,191
It does not validate the cluster count or biological identity.

90
00:13:01,191 --> 00:13:11,013
Study stability across seeds, feature sets, transformations, cluster counts, and spatial weights before assigning any biological label.

91
00:13:11,363 --> 00:13:18,481
The shallow neural workflow asks whether a compact nonlinear predictor improves held-out performance.

92
00:13:18,481 --> 00:13:28,066
Hidden units control representation capacity, weight decay regularizes parameters, and the seed influences initialization and splitting.

93
00:13:28,066 --> 00:13:36,031
Individual network weights are distributed across layers and should not be narrated as direct biological effects.

94
00:13:36,031 --> 00:13:49,845
Compare the model with linear or logistic baselines under identical grouped validation, repeat seeds, assess calibration, and use separate attribution methods only with their own stability checks.

95
00:13:49,845 --> 00:13:55,765
A performance gain supports nonlinear predictive structure, not a learned mechanism.

96
00:13:56,115 --> 00:14:00,014
The Workbench scalar-image path is a ridge screen.

97
00:14:00,014 --> 00:14:12,646
It relates aligned high-dimensional image or voxel features to a scalar outcome, reports standardized penalized coefficients, and evaluates held-out RMSE and MAE.

98
00:14:12,646 --> 00:14:22,626
A coefficient is a contribution to the penalized linear predictor conditional on the other included features and chosen scaling.

99
00:14:22,626 --> 00:14:30,892
Correlated pixels can divide or redistribute signal, and magnitude ranking is not a posterior probability.

100
00:14:30,892 --> 00:14:38,378
Inspect coefficient stability, residuals, masks, alignment, site effects, and penalty selection.

101
00:14:38,728 --> 00:14:53,499
The related SV-NN and ST-CAR publications estimate spatially structured effect surfaces, active regions, posterior selection or inclusion quantities, and uncertainty under explicit spatial models.

102
00:14:53,499 --> 00:14:57,946
Those are not the same as the Workbench ridge coefficients.

103
00:14:57,946 --> 00:15:04,879
Do not describe a ranked ridge feature as posterior-selected, spatially smoothed, or causal.

104
00:15:04,879 --> 00:15:10,833
Use the screen to identify predictive structure and motivate a fuller analysis.

105
00:15:10,833 --> 00:15:25,379
When the scientific target is spatial selection with posterior uncertainty, fit the appropriate publication-level model and report its priors, posterior summaries, diagnostics, and sensitivity.

106
00:15:25,729 --> 00:15:41,737
The Workbench image-to-image screen constructs separate PCA bases for aligned source and target images, regresses target scores on source scores, reconstructs target features, and reports held-out reconstruction error.

107
00:15:41,737 --> 00:15:48,859
Scores position subjects along variance directions and loadings map those directions to features.

108
00:15:48,859 --> 00:15:56,276
A factor is a mathematical direction of variation, not automatically a pathway or anatomical process.

109
00:15:56,276 --> 00:16:09,640
Interpret factors only after visualizing loadings and reconstructions, checking sign conventions and stability, and relating patterns to independently established anatomy or biology.

110
00:16:09,990 --> 00:16:20,384
The SBLF publication uses spatial Bayesian latent factors and spatially varying regression coefficients with posterior uncertainty.

111
00:16:20,384 --> 00:16:26,889
The Workbench screen uses separate principal component bases and score regression.

112
00:16:26,889 --> 00:16:30,063
These are different inferential objects.

113
00:16:30,063 --> 00:16:37,838
Use the Workbench result as a fast reconstruction and prediction screen, not as an SBLF posterior.

114
00:16:37,838 --> 00:16:45,851
Confirm identical masks, resolution, orientation, registration, feature order, and subject alignment.

115
00:16:45,851 --> 00:16:58,862
Choose factor counts without test leakage, inspect residual images and per-feature error, and use the full SBLF method when spatial posterior inference is required.

116
00:16:59,212 --> 00:17:06,421
The mediation screen fits an exposure-to-mediator model and outcome models with and without the mediator.

117
00:17:06,421 --> 00:17:11,914
The indirect effect is the product a times b in the fitted linear specification.

118
00:17:11,914 --> 00:17:21,115
The direct effect is the exposure coefficient with the mediator included, and the total effect is the exposure coefficient without it.

119
00:17:21,115 --> 00:17:27,432
A percentile bootstrap interval summarizes resampling variability under the observed design.

120
00:17:27,432 --> 00:17:34,985
It does not prove that the pathway is causally identified or that the mediator transmits a physical mechanism.

121
00:17:35,335 --> 00:17:39,471
Causal mediation requires more than a nonzero product.

122
00:17:39,471 --> 00:18:00,073
Exposure must precede mediator and mediator precede outcome; consistency and positivity must be plausible; exposure-outcome, exposure-mediator, and mediator-outcome confounding must be addressed; and model form, clustering, and exposure-mediator interaction may matter.

123
00:18:00,073 --> 00:18:04,055
Use sensitivity analysis for unmeasured confounding.

124
00:18:04,055 --> 00:18:11,255
Without these conditions, say the data are consistent with a model-based indirect association.

125
00:18:11,255 --> 00:18:18,837
Reserve causal mediation language for a design and analysis that explicitly support identification.

126
00:18:19,187 --> 00:18:28,505
The shape path resamples ordered contours, removes translation and scale, and applies PCA to the landmark-coordinate vectors.

127
00:18:28,505 --> 00:18:33,052
A component score positions a contour along a geometric mode.

128
00:18:33,052 --> 00:18:42,967
A loading describes coordinated landmark movement, and explained variance measures geometric variability within the processed sample.

129
00:18:42,967 --> 00:18:48,335
It does not measure aggressiveness, prognosis, or biological importance.

130
00:18:48,335 --> 00:18:59,069
Interpret each mode by plotting mean shape plus and minus component multiples, and report the preprocessing choices that define the shape space.

131
00:18:59,419 --> 00:19:03,809
Shape components can be dominated by technical correspondence.

132
00:19:03,809 --> 00:19:16,130
Verify clockwise or counterclockwise contour direction, starting-point alignment, landmark correspondence, segmentation repeatability, and treatment of incomplete boundaries.

133
00:19:16,130 --> 00:19:24,131
State whether rotation was standardized; the Workbench does not automatically remove every rotational difference.

134
00:19:24,131 --> 00:19:29,654
Decide whether orientation is a biological feature or an acquisition artifact.

135
00:19:29,654 --> 00:19:39,142
Any link between a shape score and disease or outcome belongs in a separate model with grouped validation and independent replication.

136
00:19:39,492 --> 00:19:43,719
Use this structure in meetings, reports, and manuscripts.

137
00:19:43,719 --> 00:19:51,060
We analyzed the stated observational unit using the named workflow to estimate a specific quantity.

138
00:19:51,060 --> 00:19:58,772
Under the recorded settings and validation design, the estimate was the reported value with uncertainty.

139
00:19:58,772 --> 00:20:02,182
This supports a precise statistical statement.

140
00:20:02,182 --> 00:20:07,892
It is consistent with, but does not establish, a named biological hypothesis.

141
00:20:07,892 --> 00:20:23,093
Then list the main alternative explanations and the exact independent cohort, blocked validation, perturbation, orthogonal assay, functional experiment, or prospective design required for a stronger claim.

142
00:20:23,443 --> 00:20:28,420
Language can overstate evidence even when the computation is correct.

143
00:20:28,420 --> 00:20:32,460
Replace proves interaction with conditional association.

144
00:20:32,460 --> 00:20:42,269
Replace causal voxel with screening coefficient, or with posterior-selected region only when a full posterior method supports that term.

145
00:20:42,269 --> 00:20:47,751
Replace true cell state with algorithmic cluster until externally validated.

146
00:20:47,751 --> 00:20:52,151
Replace learned mechanism with held-out predictive structure.

147
00:20:52,151 --> 00:20:59,148
Replace mediated pathway with model-based indirect effect unless causal assumptions are defended.

148
00:20:59,148 --> 00:21:05,495
These substitutions retain scientific value while making the evidence boundary explicit.

149
00:21:05,845 --> 00:21:23,422
Before presenting a result, verify that design and preprocessing are documented, the estimand and method-specific quantity are named correctly, and uncertainty, diagnostics, sensitivity, and sampling hierarchy have been reviewed.

150
00:21:23,422 --> 00:21:29,026
Separate a biologically plausible hypothesis from mechanism or causation.

151
00:21:29,026 --> 00:21:34,706
State competing explanations and the follow-up needed to distinguish them.

152
00:21:34,706 --> 00:21:42,765
Archive the result bundle, fitted object, code and package versions, data checksum, and method citations.

153
00:21:42,765 --> 00:21:55,276
For study-specific interpretation, return to the full local guide and source publication and involve experts who understand the assay, tissue, disease, and design.

